2026-09-15T20:34Z claude note Measured answer to this ticket's open question, and a correction. The toggle is NOT a workaround. `sdt-sitter-last-shutdown.json` (uninstall, 20:31:54Z) holds two full off/on cycles: each is followed within two seconds by `announce phase=census`, so the forced reconciliation works and `failed` falls 13749 -> 5242 while `pending` rises 436 -> 8926 — the reclassification this ticket predicted. Then each census is followed by a `drain-start` of ~9600 dirty ids and `pending` freezes again, seven minutes on the second attempt. `completed: 0` in both `sweep-end` records: 8926 admissible candidates, not one submitted. The sitter DOES recover unaided once notifications stop (pending climbed 44 -> 389 at 20:08-20:15, which only happens if `refreshQueue()` at 176 ran), so the starvation is not permanent — it lasts as long as the source outruns the drain. Consequence for the red test: assert ADMISSION is reached, not merely that the outer loop advances; both toggles forced the outer loop round with no `admit`. Record: `verification/incidents/0796-2026-09-15-toggle-does-not-recover.md`.

2026-09-15T20:37Z claude note Attribution correction: the two off/on cycles and the uninstall in the 20:31:54Z postmortem have no established actor. A sister session on the same codebase confirmed it performed none of the three (headless, no Run JavaScript pane) and this session performed none either. The likely hand is the author at his keyboard, but that is an inference from presence, not a reading, and the incident file now says so rather than leaving a reader to assume.
2026-09-15T20:50Z claude note Mechanism correction, found by verifying this ticket's own citations during a proportionate review of PR 588. refreshQueue() is NOT reachable only from reconciliation at 176: record() calls it at 113, and the drain calls record() every iteration at 210. So the candidate queue is rebuilt continuously and state.pending was live, not a photograph — it held at 44, then 8926, because the classification was not changing, missing-source being in the failed class and not queued. Three claims fall: refreshQueue unreachable, pending frozen, and the inference that the sitter recovers unaided once the source stops, which rested on reading the 44-to-389 climb as proof the drain had emptied. That climb is just files landing and being reclassified by the drain itself. What survives is measured rather than inferred: the loop at 201 is unbounded, admission at 228+ is never reached, active stays null, completed is 0 across both sweep-ends, and no drain-end is ever emitted. Title narrowed from 'starves both reconciliation and admission' to 'starves admission'. The recovery question is open again.
