2026-09-15T23:14Z claude note Review round 1 of PR 590. Red team found the bound's own hazard: a budgeted drain can reach the candidate search with a real backlog, and leaving on an empty candidate queue is invisible to bootstrap.js's nextSweepDelayMS, which reads pending.length and never state.draining — retry Infinity, next sweep at the reconciliation deadline, up to an hour. Verified in bootstrap.js:737-748 before fixing. Repaired inside scheduler.js with the idiom the resource gate already uses ('if (dirty.size) continue'), so bootstrap.js stays untouched for 0795 and 0797. Second red test plus mutant M36; both new mutants are caught by exactly one test each, their own.
2026-09-15T23:14Z claude note Action 3 done: the census loop is genuinely bounded. host.list() returns a snapshot array, the for-of iterates that array, nothing writes to it mid-loop, and host.yield() is the first statement in the body. Unlike dirty, it cannot grow underneath the loop. No change needed.
--- body ---
## Context

`scheduler.js`'s `pump()` has one outer loop whose body runs, in order:
reconciliation (ending in `refreshQueue()`), the drain, then admission. The
drain is a nested `while (dirty.size && current())` with **no bound**: it
returns to the outer loop only when `dirty` is empty. (Line numbers were given
here and went stale within a day, twice — the fix's own comment blocks moved
them by 15 to 40 lines. Anchors are named instead.)

`invalidate(ids)` adds to `dirty` from Zotero item notifications.
When notifications arrive faster than the drain retires them — which a bulk file
sync does comfortably, since each drain iteration pays a library-wide
`host.unattached()` ("long on its own account" by its own comment) — `dirty`
never empties, and control never returns to the top of the outer loop.

## What that starves, and why the symptom is silence rather than an error

**Admission is never reached**, so `state.active` stays `null` and not one
document is submitted. The phase stays `draining` and the heartbeat keeps firing
(`busy` is true), so nothing anywhere reports a fault. The panel shows a
plausible steady state.

**Reconciliation is not starved**, contrary to this ticket's first version and
its title until 2026-09-15. `refreshQueue()` is called from `record()` as well
as from reconciliation, and the drain calls `record()` every
iteration, so the candidate queue is rebuilt continuously and
`state.pending` is live. It held at 44, and later at 8 926, because the
classification was not changing — not because it could not be recomputed.

## Measured, on the author's library

| | |
|---|---|
| `drain-start`, 19:39:10Z | `pending` (= `dirty.size`) **761** |
| `state.draining`, ~19:57Z | **6495** |
| +~1 min | **6543** |
| +~1 min | **6573** |
| throughout 19:39 → 20:00 | `pending` 44, `active` null, `busy` true, no `drain-end` |

`storage/` went from 688 attachment folders at 19:39 to 6 168 at 19:58. The
backlog grew 8.5× while the drain ran; the candidate queue did not move once.

Record: `verification/incidents/0795-2026-09-15-drain-during-sync.md`.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `pump()`'s outer loop, and inside it in
  order: reconciliation, the drain, the candidate search, admission. Also
  `refreshQueue()`, `record()` and `invalidate()`
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`, `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py`

## Test

Red step, in the scheduler suite, and it is the whole ticket: a mock host that
adds to `dirty` on every `unattached()` call — the shape of a sync firing
notifications while the drain queries — must still reach admission. Against
today's code the test hangs or times out with `active === null`; that failure is
the positive control and must be seen before any fix.

A second assertion worth pinning in the same test: `state.pending` must change
when a mock item becomes indexable mid-drain. This already holds today — the
drain's own `record()` refreshes the queue — so it is a regression guard on a
property a bound must not break, not a red step.

## Actions

1. Write the red test above; confirm it fails against current `main`.
2. Bound the inner drain — a budget of wall-clock per outer iteration, not of
   ids: the cost bounded is a library-wide `unattached()`, so the same id count
   is a different duration on a library of 700 and one of 10 000, and this
   backlog moved 761 → 9 727 in one evening. The budget's value is a design
   number and belongs in `plugins/sdt-sitter/DESIGN-NOTES.md`, NOT in `SPEC.md`
   §5.2.9 — see the amended exit criterion below.
3. Check the same shape elsewhere in `pump()`: the census loop also iterates a
   host-supplied list, though it yields per item and is bounded by `ids.length`,
   which does not grow mid-loop. DONE, 2026-09-16: re-read at the fix, and the
   bound holds. `const ids = await host.list()` is a snapshot array taken before
   the loop, `for (const id of ids)` iterates that array and nothing writes to
   it, and `await host.yield()` is the first statement in the body. Nothing
   there can grow under the loop the way `dirty` does. No change needed.

## Invariants

- 0792's phase labelling and 0745's blocked-phase backoff keep their behaviour.
- The drain must still retire every dirty id eventually; bounding it per
  iteration must not drop work.
- 0759 stands: the totals stay live and disclosed.

## Relation to 0795

0795 pauses the sitter while Zotero syncs, which removes the most common
trigger. It does not fix this: any sustained notification source outruns the
drain the same way. 0795 can ship first and independently; this ticket is the
one that makes the loop safe.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Red test in the scheduler suite reproducing the starvation, failing
      against current `main` before the fix, and asserting that **admission is
      reached** — the 2026-09-15 postmortem shows the outer loop can be forced
      round twice, by a switch toggle, with `completed: 0` both times
- [ ] The inner drain is bounded and the outer loop demonstrably advances under
      a sustained `invalidate()` source
- [ ] `state.pending` still updates while a drain backlog exists (true today; a
      bound on the drain must not cost it)
- [ ] Any new budget constant is documented outside the source that carries it,
      in `plugins/sdt-sitter/DESIGN-NOTES.md` — amended 2026-09-15, this
      criterion said `SPEC.md` §5.2.9 and that was wrong: §5.2.9 is the query
      pipeline's disk and RAM budgets (passage counts, token geometry, embedder
      residency), a different subsystem with no sitter-scheduler constant in it.
      DESIGN-NOTES.md is where the sitter's other per-call-site constants are
      already discussed, and its open question 6 asks exactly what this constant
      is an instance of, so the record and the answer land together
- [ ] `make check` passes
